# UniMM: Enabling Automated and Unified Memory Model Description

We provide the ModelTable sets for x86, SC, ARMv8, and RISC-V generated by UniMM (in the `ModelTable` directory), along with the correctness proofs of the Memory Model Gap-Bridging Widget and Compliance Testing Widget (in the `proofs` directory).

## ModelTable

In the `ModelTable` directory, each subdirectory contains the ModelTable set of a specific memory model. 

Within each subdirectory, several CSV files are provided to describe the memory model. In each table, a cell marked with `V` indicates that the corresponding pair of memory accesses is ordered, while an empty cell indicates that reordering may occur. `basic-table-sa.csv` represents the basic ModelTable for same-address memory access pairs in the model, and `basic-table-da.csv` represents the basic ModelTable for different-address memory access pairs. The remaining CSV files are Additional Relation ModelTables. Since additional relations (such as fences and dependencies) typically enforce ordering in the same way for both same-address and different-address accesses, no separate CSV files are provided for same-address (`sa`) and different-address (`da`).


## proofs

The `proofs` directory contains the correctness proofs for the memory model gap-bridging scheme from x86 to RISC-V, as well as for the RISC-V ModelTables used in compliance testing.

In this directory, `riscv.v` specifies the RISC-V Cat model using [Rocq Prover](https://rocq-prover.org/), and `x86.v` specifies the x86 Cat model. 

For the gap-bridging scheme from x86 to RISC-V, `riscv_mt_subset.v` proves that the RISC-V ModelTables are a subset of its Cat model, while `x86_mt_equiv.v` proves that the x86 ModelTables are equivalent to its Cat model. As shown by Algorithm 4 in the paper, the gap-bridging scheme is obtained by subtracting the RISC-V ModelTables from the x86 ModelTables, thereby imposing stricter ordering constraints than actually required. In other words, the gap-bridging scheme does not compromise the correctness of program execution on RISC-V.

`riscv_mt_compliance_testing.v` proves the correctness of using the RISC-V ModelTables for compliance testing.

### Checking proofs

- [Install Rocq Prover](https://rocq-prover.org/install#linux-vscode).
- Run `cd proofs; make`. If `make` completes successfully, it indicates that the proofs are correct.